    "contents" : "\\documentclass[12pt,english]{article}\n\n\\usepackage{graphics, color}\n\\usepackage{natbib} % \\usepackage{ragged2e}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{setspace}\n\\usepackage{boondox-cal}\n\\usepackage[colorlinks=false, linkcolor = black]{hyperref} % draft turns off -- hyperlinks\\usepackage[draft, colorlinks=false, linkcolor = black]{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{footnote}\n\\usepackage{csquotes}\n\\usepackage{pdflscape}\n\\usepackage{fullpage} % \\usepackage{threeparttable}\n\\usepackage{float}\n\\usepackage{array}% http://ctan.org/pkg/array\n\n\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\hypersetup{\n    colorlinks,\n    linkcolor={red!50!black},\n    citecolor={blue!50!black},\n    urlcolor={blue!80!black}\n}\n\n\n\\usepackage{exercise}\n\\newcommand{\\mydate}{Updated: }\n\\renewcommand{\\ExerciseName}{Example}\n\n\n\\usepackage[protrusion=true,expansion=true]{microtype}\t\n\n% --------------------------------------------------------------------\n% Definitions (do not change this)\n% --------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\newcommand{\\HRule}[1]{\\rule{\\linewidth}{#1}} \t% Horizontal rule\n\n\\makeatletter\t\t\t\t\t\t\t% Title\n\\def\\printtitle{%\t\t\t\t\t\t\n    {\\centering \\@title\\par}}\n\\makeatother\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n\n\\makeatletter\t\t\t\t\t\t\t% Author\n\\def\\printauthor{%\t\t\t\t\t\n    {\\centering \\large \\@author}}\t\t\t\t\n\\makeatother\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n\n% --------------------------------------------------------------------\n% Metadata (Change this)\n% --------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\title{\t\\normalsize \\textsc{} \t% Subtitle\n\t\t \t\\\\[2.0cm]\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t% 2cm spacing\n\t\t\t\\HRule{0.5pt} \\\\\t\t\t\t\t\t% Upper rule\n\t\t\t\\LARGE \\textbf{\\uppercase{Multivariate Hypothesis Testing in \\textbf{R}}}\t% Title\n\t\t\t\\HRule{2pt} \\\\ [0.5cm]\t\t% Lower rule + 0.5cm spacing\n\t\t\t\\normalsize{\\mydate \\today}\t\t\t% Todays date\n\t\t}\n\n\\author{\n\t\tKen Aho\\\\\t\n\t\tDepartment of Biological Sciences\\\\\t\n\t\tIdaho State University\\\\\n        \\texttt{ahoken@isu.edu} \\\\\n}\n\n% \\title{}\n% \\author{Ken Aho \\\\\\\\\n% Idaho State University}\n% %  Department of Biological Sciences}\n% %  Idaho State University\\\\}\n% \\date{\\today}\n\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\thispagestyle{empty}\t\t% Remove page numbering on this page\n\n\\printtitle\t\t\t\t\t% Print the title data as defined above\n  \t\\vfill\n\\printauthor\t\t\t\t% Print the author data as defined above\n\\newpage\n% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n% Begin document\n% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\setcounter{page}{1}\t\t% Set page numbering to begin on this page\n\n\n<<echo = F>>=\nopts_chunk$set(fig.path='figure/minimal-', fig.align='center', fig.show='hold', concordance=TRUE, comment = NA)\noptions(formatR.arrow=TRUE)\noptions(width = 75)\n@\n\n\\thispagestyle{empty}\n\\afterpage{\\null\\newpage}\n\\setcounter{page}{1}\n\n\\tableofcontents\n%\\newpage\n\\vspace{1in}\n\n \\section{Multivariate General Linear Models}\nIn terms of inferential procedures we can distinguish univariate from multivariate analyses in a simple way. Multivariate analyses have multiple response variables whereas univariate analyses have a single response variable.  Most datasets collected by biologists are multivariate, although they will generally be subset to allow univariate analyses.  Multivariate analyses are particularly prevalent in community ecology where the effect of explanatory variables on collections of species is often of interest.\n\n \n \n % It should be emphasized that just as a significant test for the effect of the $k$th parameter in a multiple regression does not guarantee significance of the same parameter in a simple linear regression (or vice versa), a significant result for $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ with respect to $\\boldsymbol{X}$ in a multivariate test does not guarantee a significant result in univariate tests of individual $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ variables with respect to $\\boldsymbol{X}$ (or vice versa).  \n \n \\subsection{Multivariate Data and Summary Statistics}\n Before describing analysis procedures it is prudent to start with a look at multivariate data and its common summary statistics.  Consider a single factor level with length $n$, from a single factor for a dataset of $d$ response variables, $Y_1, Y_2, \\dots, Y_d$.  The complete $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ matrix will have dimension $n \\times d$ (i.e., $n$ rows and $d$ columns).\n \n A vector of dependent variable population means associated with the $\\ell$ th factor level, $\\ell \\in \\{1, 2, \\dots, a\\}$ can be denoted as:\n \\begin{equation}\n \\boldsymbol{\\mu_{\\ell}}= \\left[ \\begin{matrix}\n    {{\\mu }_{1}}  \\\\\n    {{\\mu }_{2}}  \\\\\n    \\vdots   \\\\\n    {{\\mu }_{d}}  \\\\\n \\end{matrix} \\right].\\end{equation}\n \n The population mean vector is estimated with the sample mean vector, $\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\ell}}$:\n \\begin{equation}\n \\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\ell}}= \\left[ \\begin{matrix}\n    {{{\\bar{X}}}_{1}}  \\\\\n    {{{\\bar{X}}}_{2}}  \\\\\n    \\vdots   \\\\\n    {{{\\bar{X}}}_{d}}  \\\\\n \\end{matrix} \\right].\\end{equation}\n \n \\noindent In $\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\ell}}$, $\\bar{X_1}$  will be the sample mean of the subset of $Y_1$ data associated with the $\\ell$th factor level. \n \n Associated with each factor level or combination of factor levels in $\\boldsymbol{X}$ is a $d \\times d$ dispersion matrix, called the \\textbf{covariance matrix}.  The population covariance matrix associated with the $\\ell$th factor level, denoted $\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma_{\\ell}}$, will contain on its diagonal population variances associated with each of the $d$ response variables.  Off diagonal elements will contain the $(d^2 - d)/2$ population covariances.  \n \n \\begin{equation}\n \\boldsymbol{\\Sigma_{\\ell}} = \\left[ \\begin{matrix}\n    {{\\sigma }_{11}} & {{\\sigma }_{21}} & \\vdots  & {{\\sigma }_{d1}}  \\\\\n    {{\\sigma }_{12}} & {{\\sigma }_{22}} & \\vdots  & {{\\sigma }_{d2}}  \\\\\n    \\ldots  & \\cdots  & \\ddots  & \\ldots   \\\\\n    {{\\sigma }_{1d}} & {{\\sigma }_{2d}} & \\vdots  & {{\\sigma }_{dd}}  \\\\\n \\end{matrix} \\right].\\end{equation}\n \n The matrix $\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma_{\\ell}}$  can be estimated with $\\boldsymbol{S_{\\ell}}$:\n \\begin{equation}\n \\boldsymbol{S_{\\ell}} = \n \\left[ \\begin{matrix}\n   {{S}_{11}} & {{S}_{21}} & \\vdots  & \\text{ }{{\\text{S}}_{d1}}  \\\\\n    {{S}_{12}} & {{S}_{22}} & \\vdots  & {{S}_{d2}}  \\\\\n    \\ldots  & \\cdots  & \\ddots  & \\ldots   \\\\\n    {{S}_{1d}} & {{S}_{2d}} & \\vdots  & {{S}_{dd}}  \\\\\n \\end{matrix}\\right].\\end{equation}\n \n Note that the covariance element $S_{12}$ in $\\boldsymbol{S_{\\ell}}$ will be equivalent to $S_{21}$.  As we know the formula for the sample variance for variable $Y_1$ is:\n \n \\begin{equation}\n S_{1}^{2}={{S}_{11}}=\\frac{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n}{{{({{X}_{i1}}-{{{\\bar{X}}}_{1}})}^{2}}}}{n-1}.\n \\end{equation}\n \n Recall that the covariances are a measure of how random variables vary with respect to each other.  The covariance of variable 1 and 2 is estimated using\n \n \n \\begin{equation}\n {{S}_{12}}=\\frac{1}{n-1}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n}{({{X}_{i1}}-}{{\\bar{X}}_{1}})({{X}_{i2}}-{{\\bar{X}}_{2}}).\n \\end{equation}\n \n We can derive the sample covariance matrix in R using the either the function \\texttt{cov} or \\texttt{var}.\n \n <<>>=\n Y1 <- rnorm(20)\n Y2 <- rnorm(20)\n \n cov(cbind(Y1, Y2)); var(cbind(Y1, Y2))\n @\n \n The population \\textbf{correlation} (standardized covariance) matrix for the $\\ell$th factor level will have the form:\n \n \\begin{equation}\n  \\boldsymbol{\\rho_{\\ell}}=\n \\left[\\begin{matrix}\n    1 & \\rho_{21} & \\vdots  & \\rho_{d1}  \\\\\n    \\rho_{12} & 1 & \\vdots  & {{\\rho}_{d2}}  \\\\\n    \\ldots  & \\cdots  & \\ddots  & \\ldots   \\\\\n    {{\\rho}_{1d}} & {{\\rho}_{2d}} & \\vdots  & 1  \\\\\n \\end{matrix} \\right],\n \\end{equation}\n \n whereas the sample correlation matrix for the $\\ell$th factor level has the form:\n \\begin{equation}\n \\boldsymbol{R_{\\ell}}=\n \\left[ \\begin{matrix}\n    1 & {{R}_{21}} & \\vdots  & R_{d1}  \\\\\n    {{R}_{12}} & 1 & \\vdots  & {{R}_{d2}}  \\\\\n    \\ldots  & \\cdots  & \\ddots  & \\ldots   \\\\\n    {{R}_{1d}} & {{R}_{2d}} & \\vdots  & 1  \\\\\n \\end{matrix} \\right].\n \\end{equation}\n \n A sample correlation of two variables can be calculated from their sample variances and covariance.\n \n \\begin{equation}{{R}_{12}}=\\frac{{{S}_{12}}}{\\sqrt{{{S}_{11}}}\\sqrt{{{S}_{22}}}}.\\end{equation}\n \n Ones will occur on the diagonal of all correlation matrices since a variable will always be perfectly correlated with itself.  We can derive sample correlation matrices in R using the function \\texttt{cor} or by applying \\texttt{cov2cor} to a covariance matrix:\n \n <<>>=\n cvm <- cov(cbind(Y1, Y2))\n cov2cor(cvm)\n @\n \n \\subsection{Hotelling's $T^2$ test}\n \\textbf{Hotelling's $T^2$ test} \\citep{hotelling1931} can be considered a multivariate analog of a $t$-test since it quantifies the effect of a single factor with two factor levels on a $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ matrix of interest. The null hypothesis will be that the two population mean vectors are equal, i.e.:  \n \\vspace{0.01in}\n \\begin{center} H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_1} = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_2}$\n \\end{center}\n \\begin{center}\n H$_\\text{A}$: $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_1} \\neq \\boldsymbol{\\mu_2}$\n \\end{center}\n \n \n The test statistic is calculated over several steps.  First we let:\n \\begin{equation}\n {{T}^{2}}=\\frac{{{n}_{1}}{{n}_{2}}}{({{n}_{1}}+{{n}_{2}})}D_{{}}^{2}\n \\end{equation}\n \n where $D^2$ is the \\textbf{Mahalanobis distance} of the factor levels assuming a pooled variance covariance matrix: \n \\begin{equation}\n D^2 = \\left(\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{1}-\\bar{X}_{2}}\\right)'\\boldsymbol{S^{-1}}\\left(\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{1}-\\bar{X}_{2}}\\right)\n \\end{equation}\n where\n \\begin{equation}\n \\boldsymbol{S} = \\frac{1}{{{n}_{1}}+{{n}_{2}}-2}[(n_1 - 1)S_1 + (n_2 - 1)S_2].\n \\end{equation}\n The test statistic is:\n \\begin{equation}\nF^*=\\frac{{{n}_{1}}+{{n}_{2}}-\\left(d+1 \\right)}{\\left( {{n}_{1}}+{{n}_{2}}-2 \\right)d}{{T}^{2}}\n \\end{equation}\n If the null hypothesis is true then $F^* \\sim F[d, n_1 + n_2 - (d + 1)]$, and a $P$-value will be calculated as $P(F \\geq F^*)$.  \n \\begin{Exercise}\n A dataset collected by \\citet{aho2006} concerned the distribution of communities of lichens and vascular and avascular plant species on montane cliffs in Northeast Yellowstone National Park.  Of particular interest was whether substrate (limestone or andesitic conglomerate) influenced community composition.  We are interested in the hypotheses:  \n \\vspace{0.01in}\n \\begin{center} H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_L} = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_C}$\n \\end{center}\n \\begin{center} H$_\\text{A}$: $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_L} \\neq \\boldsymbol{\\mu_C}$\n \\end{center}\n \n A subset of the community data, and associated environmental data  can be found in the dataframes \\texttt{cliffs.sp} and \\texttt{cliff.env} in the package \\textit{plant.ecol}.  The \\textit{plant.ecol} package is currently archived on the Aho lab webpage \\url{https://sites.google.com/a/isu.edu/aho/} (near the bottom). \n \n <<>>=\n library(plant.ecol)\n data(cliff.sp)\n data(cliff.env)\n Y <- cliff.sp\n X <- cliff.env[,1]\n @\n \n There are $n = 54$ sites and $d = 11$ species describing the sites.\n \n <<>>=\n dim(Y)\n d <- 11\n @\n \n There are 27 observations for both the andesite and limestone factor levels. \n \n <<>>=\n Xn<-as.numeric(factor(X))\n n1 <- length(X[Xn == 1])\n n2 <- length(X[Xn == 2])\n n1; n2\n @\n \n Here is the pooled covariance matrix.\n \n <<>>=\n S<-(1/(n1+n2-2))*(((n1-1)*(var(Y[Xn==1,]))+((n2-1) * var(Y[Xn==2,]))))\n S\n @\n \n Here is the Mahalanobis distance:\n \n <<>>=\n X1 <- apply(Y[Xn==1,], 2, mean)\n X2 <- apply(Y[Xn==2,], 2, mean)\n D2 <- (X1 - X2) %*% solve(S) %*% (X1 - X2)\n D2\n @\n \n Here is the test statistic and $P$-value:\n \n <<>>=\n T2 <- (n1 * n2)/(n1 + n2) * D2\n F.star <- ((n1 + n2 - (d + 1))/((n1 + n2 - 2) * d)) * T2\n p.val <- pf(F.star, d, (n1 + n2 - (d + 1)), lower.tail = FALSE)\n p.val\n @\n \n The entire analysis can be run using the function \\texttt{Hotelling} from \\textit{plant.ecol}.\n \n <<>>=\n Hotelling(cliff.sp,cliff.env[,1])\n @\n \n Or with \\texttt{lm} and \\texttt{anova} or \\texttt{Anova} in \\textit{car}.  The latter will be necessary for unbalanced multifactor designs.  The functions \\texttt{anova} and \\texttt{Anova} will give identical answers for one way designs and/or balanced designs.  \n <<>>=\n lm.cliff <- lm(as.matrix(cliff.sp) ~ cliff.env[,1])\n anova(lm.cliff)\n \n library(car)\n Anova(lm.cliff)\n \n @\n \n These approaches utilize an MANOVA approach, described next.\n \n \\end{Exercise}\n \n \\subsection{One Way MANOVA}\n When we have multiple $Y$s with respect to a factor with at least two levels or we have multiple factors \\textbf{Multivariate Analysis of Variance} (\\textbf{MANOVA}) can be used for inferential tests.  Simplistically, a MANOVA is an ANOVA with several quantitative response variables instead of just one.  Further, just as a one way ANOVA, which allows inferences concerning two factor level populations, is equivalent to a pooled variance $t$-test with a two tailed alternative, a one way MANOVA with two factor levels will be an equivalent analysis to Hotelling's $T^2$ test.  My notation here largely follows \\citep{john}. \n \n The null hypothesis for a one way MANOVA with $a$ factor levels in factor $A$, is that the population mean vectors \n ($\\boldsymbol{\\mu_{\\ell}}$'s, $\\ell \\in \\{1,2,\\dots,a\\}$) describing the subsets of the different factor levels are equal.  That is: \n \n \\begin{center} H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_1} = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_2} = \\dots = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_a}$\n \\end{center}\n \\begin{center}H$_\\text{A}$: At least one $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_{\\ell}}$ is different from the others\n \\end{center}\n\n Or, alternatively\n \n \\begin{center} H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\tau_1} = \\boldsymbol{\\tau_2} = \\dots = \\boldsymbol{\\tau_a} = \\boldsymbol{0}$\n \\end{center}\n \\begin{center} H$_\\text{A}$: At least one $\\boldsymbol{\\tau_{\\ell}} \\neq = \\boldsymbol{0}$ \n \\end{center}\n\n \n where $\\boldsymbol{\\tau_{\\ell}}$ is the vector of effect sizes for the ${\\ell}$th factor level.\n \nAs with ANOVA, MANOVA compares variance measures based on partitioning sums of squares.  The sums of squares, however, now have a multivariate form and are called \\textbf{sum of squares and cross product matrices} or \\textbf{SSCP matrices}.  The \\textit{between} factor levels SSCP matrix is calculated as:\n \n \\begin{equation}\\label{eq:A}\n \\boldsymbol{A} = \\sum_{\\ell=1}^a n_{\\ell} \\times \\left(\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\ell}-\\bar{X}}\\right)\\left(\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\ell}-\\bar{X}}\\right)'\n \\end{equation}\n  \n where $\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}}$ is the overall sample mean vector across all factor levels and $n_{\\ell}$ is the number of observations in the $\\ell$th factor level.\n The \\textit{within} factor levels SSCP matrix is calculated as:\n \n  \\begin{equation}\\label{eq:E}\n \\boldsymbol{E} = \\sum_{\\ell=1}^a \\sum_{j=1}^{n_{\\ell}} \n \\left(\\boldsymbol{X_{j \\ell}-\\bar{X_{\\ell}}}\\right)\n \\left(\\boldsymbol{X_{j \\ell}-\\bar{X_{\\ell}}}\\right)'\n  \\end{equation}\n \n where $\\boldsymbol{X_{\\ell j}}$ is the $j$th vector of observations, $j \\in \\{1,2,\\dots, n_{\\ell}\\}$, from the ${\\ell}$th factor level.  The \\textit{total} SSCP matrix is simply: \n \n  \\begin{equation}\n \\boldsymbol{T} = \\boldsymbol{A} + \\boldsymbol{E}\n  \\end{equation}\n  \n The degrees of freedom for $\\boldsymbol{A}$, $\\boldsymbol{E}$, and $\\boldsymbol{T}$ are $a - 1$, $\\sum_{\\ell =1}^{a}{{{n}_{\\ell}}-a}$, and $\\sum_{\\ell =1}^{a}{{{n}_{\\ell }}-1}$, respectively. Table \\ref{tab:MANOVA1way} summarizes this information.\n \n {\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.5}\n \\begin{table}[H]\n  \\centering\n  \\caption{One-way MANOVA table.}\n  \\label{tab:MANOVA1way}\n  \\vspace{0.1in}\n  \\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|}\n  \\hline\n Source of variation & df                                    & Sums of squares and cross product matrices                                                                                                                                                             \\\\\\hline\n Between             & $a - 1$                               & $\\boldsymbol{A} = \\sum_{\\ell=1}^a n_{\\ell} \\times \\left(\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\ell}-\\bar{X}}\\right)\\left(\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\ell}-\\bar{X}}\\right)'$                        \\\\\n Within (Error)      & $\\sum_{\\ell =1}^{a}{{{n}_{\\ell}}-a}$  & $\\boldsymbol{E} = \\sum_{\\ell=1}^a \\sum_{j=1}^{n_{\\ell}} \\left(\\boldsymbol{X_{j \\ell}-\\bar{X_{\\ell}}}\\right)\\left(\\boldsymbol{X_{j \\ell}-\\bar{X_{\\ell}}}\\right)'$ \\\\\n Total               & $\\sum_{\\ell =1}^{a}{{{n}_{\\ell }}-1}$ & $\\boldsymbol{T} = \\boldsymbol{A} + \\boldsymbol{E}$  \\\\\n \\hline\n \\end{tabular}}\n \\end{table}\n \n Notice the similarity to a univariate ANOVA table (except that now sums of squares are contained in matrices).  \n \n \\subsubsection{Test Statistics}\n \n There are four commonly used test statistics for assessing the equality of population mean vectors given this information.\n They include \\textbf{Wilk's lambda}:\n \n  \\begin{equation}\\label{Eq:ts1}\n  \\Lambda^* = \\frac{|\\boldsymbol{E}|}{|\\boldsymbol{A}+\\boldsymbol{E}|} = \\frac{|\\boldsymbol{E}|}{|\\boldsymbol{T}|}, \n  \\end{equation}\n \n \\textbf{Pillai's trace}:\n \n  \\begin{equation}\\label{Eq:ts2}\n \\Phi^* = tr[(\\boldsymbol{E}+\\boldsymbol{A})^{-1}\\boldsymbol{A}],\t\n  \\end{equation}\n \n \\textbf{Hotelling-Lawley's trace}:\n \n  \\begin{equation}\n \\Theta^* = tr(\\boldsymbol{E}^{-1}\\boldsymbol{A}),\t\n  \\end{equation}\n \n and \\textbf{Roy's largest root}:\n \n  \\begin{equation}\\label{Eq:ts4}\n \\Upsilon^* = \\frac{\\lambda_1}{\\lambda_1+ 1}.\t\n  \\end{equation}\n \n  In equations \\ref{Eq:ts1}-\\ref{Eq:ts4} the notation $|*|$ means \"find the determinant of $*$, $tr(*)$ means find the trace of $*$, and $\\lambda_1$ is the first eigenvalue from an eigendecomposition of the symmetric matrix $\\boldsymbol{E^{-1}A}$.  \n  \n  Wilk's lambda must be further adapted to calculate test statistics.  Table \\ref{tab:wilks} shows sampling distributions for Wilk's Lambda based on spacial cases for the number of response variables, $d$, and the number of factor levels, $a$.\n  \n  {\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{2}\n  \\begin{table}[H]\n   \\centering\n   \\caption{Sampling distribution under H$_0$ for transformations of Wilk's lambda.}\n   \\label{tab:wilks}\n   \\vspace{0.1in}\n   \\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}\n   \\hline\n  $d$     & $a$     &  Transformation of $\\Lambda^*$ and sampling distribution                                                                                            \\\\\\hline\n  1       & $\\geq2$ & $\\left( \\frac{\\sum{{{n}_{\\ell }}-a}}{a-1}\\right)\\left( \\frac{1-\\Lambda^*}{\\Lambda^*} \\right)\\sim F(a-1,\\sum{{{n}_{\\ell }}-a})$ \\\\\n  2       & $\\geq2$ &  $\\left( \\frac{\\sum{{{n}_{\\ell }}-a-1}}{a-1}\\right)\\left( \\frac{1-\\sqrt{\\Lambda^*}}{\\sqrt{\\Lambda^*}} \\right)\\sim F[{2(a-1),2\\left(\\sum{{{n}_{\\ell }}-a}-1 \\right)}]$                                                                                                                             \\\\\n  $\\geq1$ & 2       &    $\\left( \\frac{\\sum{{{n}_{\\ell }}-d-1}}{d}\\right)\\left( \\frac{1-\\sqrt{\\Lambda *}}{\\sqrt{\\Lambda *}} \\right)\\sim {{F}({d,\\sum{{{n}_{\\ell }}-d}-1}})$                                                                                                                           \\\\\n  $\\geq1$ & 3       &   $\\left( \\frac{\\sum{{{n}_{\\ell }}-d-2}}{d}\n  \\right)\\left( \\frac{1-\\sqrt{\\Lambda *}}{\\sqrt{\\Lambda *}} \\right)\\sim{{F}({2d,2\\left( \\sum{{{n}_{\\ell }}-d}-1 \\right)}})$ \\\\\n  \\hline\n  \\end{tabular}\n  \\end{table}}\n  \n  For large sample sizes and other cases, a modification of  $\\Lambda^*$ was recommended by \\citep{bartlett1938}, who showed that if H$_0$ is true and $\\sum n_{\\ell}=n$ is large:\n  \n  \\begin{equation}\n  -\\left( n-1-\\frac{\\left( d+a \\right)}{2} \\right)\\ln \\left( \\frac{\\left| \\boldsymbol{E} \\right|}{|\\boldsymbol{T}|} \\right))\\sim \\chi^2[d(a - 1)]\n  \\end{equation}\n  \n   As with Wilk's lambda, Eqs. \\ref{Eq:ts2}-\\ref{Eq:ts4} will be considered with respect to a null $F$-distribution to calculate $P$-values, although degrees of freedom will vary with the test statistic methodology. Of the four test statistics, Pillai's trace tends to be the most forgiving of violations of multivariate normality.  Because Roy's largest root constitutes an upper bound for the $F$-test statistic under H$_0$, it will give a lower bound estimate for $P(data|\\text{H}_0)$.  Thus, Roy's largest root is generally disregarded when it's result is significant but the other three perspectives are not significant.  All four methods will converge to the same $P$-value given large sample sizes.  \n   \n    \\begin{Exercise}\n    Let's look at an example of how we might calculate MANOVA SSCP matrices for a hypothetical dataset.  In Table \\ref{tab:ex1} $\\boldsymbol{Y_1}$ and $\\boldsymbol{Y_2}$ are plant height in cm and plant weight in g, respectively.  $\\boldsymbol{X}$  represents categorical nitrogen levels in the soil.  We are interested in the quantifying evidence concerning the null hypothesis: H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_{1}}=\\boldsymbol{\\mu _{2}}=\\boldsymbol{\\mu_{3}}$.\n    \n    \n    \\begin{table}[H]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Hypothetical multivariate vegetation dataset}\n    \\label{tab:ex1}\n    \\vspace{0.1in}\n    \\begin{tabular}{ccc}\n    \\hline\n    $\\boldsymbol{Y_1}$ & $\\boldsymbol{Y_2}$ & $\\boldsymbol{X}$ \\\\ \\hline\n    9                  & 3                  & Hi               \\\\\n    6                  & 2                  & Hi               \\\\\n    9                  & 7                  & Hi               \\\\\n    0                  & 0                  & Lo               \\\\\n    2                  & 0.5                & Lo               \\\\\n    NA                 & NA                 & Lo               \\\\\n    3                  & 8                  & Med              \\\\\n    1                  & 9                  & Med              \\\\\n    2                  & 7                  & Med             \\\\ \\hline\n    \\end{tabular}\n    \\end{table}\n    \n    We have:\n    \n    <<tidy = T>>=\n    Y1<-c(9,6,9,0,2,NA,3,1,2)\n    Y2<-c(3,2,7,0,0.5,NA,8,9,7)\n    X<-c(rep(\"Hi\",3),rep(\"Lo\",3),rep(\"Med\",3))\n    @\n   \n   Note that we have two response variables and a single factorial explanatory variable with three factor levels.  We have one missing experimental unit, equating to one plant height and weight measurement from the \"Lo\" factor level. We perform initial calculations to get mean vectors.\n    \n   Here are vectors of mean height and weight.\n   \n   <<>>=\n   Height.vector <- tapply(Y1, X, mean, na.rm = TRUE)\n   Height.vector\n   Weight.vector <- tapply(Y2, X, mean, na.rm = TRUE)\n   Weight.vector\n   @\n    \n   Here is a matrix containing all of our $\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\ell}}$ vectors:\n    \n   <<tidy = TRUE>>=\n   Xbar.1_3 <- matrix(nrow=2, ncol=3, data=rbind(Height.vector, Weight.vector), \n                       dimnames=list(c(\"Height\", \"Weight\"), \n                                     c(\"X1_Means\", \"X2_Means\", \"X3_Means\")))\n   Xbar.1_3\n   @\n   \n   Here is our grand mean vector:\n   \n   <<tidy = TRUE>>=\n   Xbar.grand <- matrix(ncol = 1, nrow = 2, data = as.matrix(apply(cbind(Y1, Y2), \n                                                                   2, mean, na.rm = T)), \n                        dimnames = list(c(\"Height\", \"Weight\"), \"Grand_Means\"))\n   \n   Xbar.grand\n   @\n   \n   Thus, our mean vectors are: \\\\ \\vspace{0.01in}\n   \\begin{center}\n   ${{\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}}_{1}}}=\\left[ \\begin{matrix}\n      8  \\\\\n      4  \\\\\n   \\end{matrix} \\right]$, \n   ${{\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}}_{2}}}=\\left[ \\begin{matrix}\n      1  \\\\\n      2  \\\\\n   \\end{matrix} \\right]$, and \n   ${{\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}}_{3}}}=\\left[ \\begin{matrix}\n      2  \\\\\n      8  \\\\\n   \\end{matrix} \\right]$,\n   \\end{center}\n   and our grand mean vector is:\n   \\begin{center}\n   ${{\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}}}}=\\left[ \\begin{matrix}\n      4  \\\\\n      4.5625  \\\\\n   \\end{matrix} \\right]$.\n   \\end{center}\n   \n   Next, we calculate the covariance and correlation matrices for the three treatments:\n   \n   <<>>=\n   Lo <- cbind(Y1[X == \"Lo\"], Y2[X == \"Lo\"])\n   Cov_Hi <- cov(cbind(Y1[X == \"Hi\"],Y2[X == \"Hi\"]))\n   Cov_Med <- cov(cbind(Y1[X == \"Med\"], Y2[X == \"Med\"]))\n   Cov_Lo <- cov(matrix(nrow = 2, ncol = 2, data = Lo[!is.na(Lo)], byrow = F))\n   \n   Cov_Hi\n   Cov_Med\n   Cov_Lo\n   @\n   \n   The variances of plant height and weight within each factor level are on the diagonals of the variance covariance matrices.  The covariance between height and weight are on the off-diagonal elements.  Here are the correlation matrices.\n   \n  \n   <<>>=\n   Cor_Hi<-cov2cor(Cov_Hi)\n   Cor_Med<-cov2cor(Cov_Med)\n   Cor_Lo<-cov2cor(Cov_Lo)\n   \n   Cor_Hi\n   Cor_Med\n   Cor_Lo\n   @\n   \n   The number of rows and columns in $\\boldsymbol{A}$ and other MANOVA sums of squares and cross product matrices (Table \\ref{tab:MANOVA1way}) will be  equivalent to the number of dependent variables, $d$, in the model.  Thus, for the current example, the $\\boldsymbol{A}$, $\\boldsymbol{E}$, and $\\boldsymbol{T}$ matrices will be $2 \\times 2$.  \n   \n   To solve for $\\boldsymbol{A}$ (Eq. \\ref{eq:A}), we need to first calculate the sum of squares for height and weight as well as the cross products for height and weight.  The cross products will be the off-diagonal elements of $\\boldsymbol{A}$, while the sums of squares will make up the diagonal. \n   \n   Here are the sums of squares for height and weight:\n   \n   <<>>=\n   n.o<-c(3,2,3)\n   SS.ht<-sum((Xbar.1_3[1,] - Xbar.grand[1])^2 * n.o) \n   SS.ht\n   SS.wt<-sum((Xbar.1_3[2,] - Xbar.grand[2])^2 * n.o)\n   SS.wt\n   @\n   \n   To calculate the cross products we first need to find the differences between each $\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\ell}}$ and $\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}}$ \n   \n   <<>>=\n   Ht.diff<-Xbar.1_3[1,]-Xbar.grand[1]\n   Wt.diff<-Xbar.1_3[2,]-Xbar.grand[2]\n   Ht.diff\n   Wt.diff\n   @\n   \n   We multiply these entities by the number of observations and take this sum to find the cross product.  The result here is a single number because, in this case, the lower triangle in $\\boldsymbol{A}$ consists of a single element and the upper triangle in the sums of squares matrices is always identical to the lower triangle.\n   \n   <<>>=\n   sum(Ht.diff * Wt.diff * n.o)\n   @\n   \n   To solve for $\\boldsymbol{E}$ (Eq. \\ref{eq:E}), we first calculate the differences between the individual observed values and the treatment mean vectors.  $\\boldsymbol{E}$  will also be a $d \\times d$ matrix consisting of both sums of squares elements and cross products.  We have:\n   \n   <<>>=\n   Ht.res1 <- Y1[X == \"Hi\"] - Xbar.1_3[1]\n   Ht.res2 <- Y1[X == \"Lo\"] - Xbar.1_3[,2][1]\n   Ht.res3 <- Y1[X == \"Med\"] - Xbar.1_3[,3][1]\n   sq.ht <- rbind(Ht.res1, Ht.res2, Ht.res3)^2\n   Ht.resSS <- sum(sq.ht[!is.na(sq.ht)])\n   Ht.resSS\n   \n   Wt.res1 <- Y2[X == \"Hi\"] - Xbar.1_3[2]\n   Wt.res2 <- Y2[X == \"Lo\"] - Xbar.1_3[,2][2]\n   Wt.res3 <- Y2[X == \"Med\"] - Xbar.1_3[,3][2]\n   \n   sq.wt <- rbind(Wt.res1, Wt.res2, Wt.res3)^2\n   Wt.resSS <- sum(sq.wt[!is.na(sq.wt)])\n   Wt.resSS\n   @\n   \n   To get the off diagonal elements of the residual SSCP matrix, we multiply the residuals of height and weight.  Again, for $d = 2$ (the current situation), this will be a single number. \n   \n   <<>>=\n   mat <- rbind(Ht.res1, Ht.res2, Ht.res3)* rbind(Wt.res1, Wt.res2, Wt.res3)\n   sum(mat[!is.na(mat)])\n   @\n   \n   Table \\ref{tab:ex2.1} contains a summary of our work so far.\n   \n    {\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{2}\n    \\begin{table}[H]\n     \\centering\n     \\caption{MANOVA table for exercise 2}\n     \\label{tab:ex2.1}\n     \\vspace{0.1in}\n     \\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|}\n     \\hline\n    Source of variation & df                                    & Sums of squares and cross product matrices                                                                                                                                                             \\\\ \\hline\n    Between             & $3 - 1 = 2$ & $ \\boldsymbol{A} = \\left[ \\begin{matrix} 78 & -1.5  \\\\  -1.5 & 73.594  \\\\ \\end{matrix} \\right]$  \\\\ \\hline\n    Within (Error)      & $8 - 3 = 5$ & $ \\boldsymbol{E} = \\left[ \\begin{matrix} 10 & 5.5  \\\\   5.5 & 16.125  \\\\ \\end{matrix} \\right]$ \\\\ \\hline\n    Total               & $8 - 1 = 7$ & $ \\boldsymbol{T} = \\left[ \\begin{matrix} 88 & 4  \\\\   4 & 89.719  \\\\ \\end{matrix} \\right]$  \\\\  \\hline\n    \\end{tabular}\n    \\end{table}}\n   \n   <<>>=\n   A <- matrix(nrow = 2, ncol = 2, data = c(78,1.5,1.5,73.594), byrow = F)\n   E <- matrix(nrow = 2, ncol = 2, data = c(10,5.5,5.5, 16.125), byrow = F)\n   T <- matrix(nrow = 2, ncol = 2, data = c(88,4,4,89.719), byrow = F)\n   @\n   \n   Here is Wilk's Lambda (Eq. \\ref{Eq:ts1}):\n   <<tidy = T>>=\n   Wilks<-det(E)/det(T)\n   Wilks\n   \n   @\n   \n   Since $d = 2$, and $a = 3$ we use $\\left( \\frac{\\sum{{{n}_{\\ell }}-g-1}}{g-1} \\right)\\left( \\frac{1-\\sqrt{\\Lambda^*}}{\\sqrt{\\Lambda^*}} \\right)$ to calculate the test statistic.\n   \n   \\[F^* = \\left( \\frac{8-3-1}{3-1} \\right)\\left( \\frac{1-\\sqrt{0.016626}}{\\sqrt{0.016626}} \\right)=13.51087\\]\n   \n   <<>>=\n   F.star <- ((8-3-1)/(3-1))*((1-sqrt(.016626))/sqrt(0.016626))\n   F.star\n   @\n   \n   The $P$-value is $P(X \\geq F^*)$, where $X \\sim F \\left[2(a-1),2\\sum n_{\\ell} - a - 1)\\right]$. Thus, for the current example $X \\sim F\\left[2 \\times 2 = 4, 2(8 - 3- 1) = 8\\right]$.  \n   \n   and we have:\n   \n   <<>>=\n   pf(F.star, 4, 8, lower.tail = F)\n   @\n   \n   This result agrees with output from the functions \\texttt{manova} and \\texttt{Manova}.\n   \n   <<>>=\n   summary(manova(lm(cbind(Y1, Y2) ~ X)), test = \"Wilks\")\n   summary(Manova(lm(cbind(Y1, Y2) ~ X)), test = \"Wilks\")\n   @\n   \n   Pillai's trace (Eq. \\ref{Eq:ts2}) is:\n   \n   <<>>=\n   sum(diag(solve(E+A)%*%A))\n   @\n   \n   <<>>=\n   summary(manova(lm(cbind(Y1, Y2) ~ X)), test = \"Pillai\")\n   @\n   \n   Results from all four approaches can be obtained simultaneously.   \n   \n   <<>>=\n   summary(Manova(lm(cbind(Y1, Y2) ~ X)))\n   @\n  \n   \\end{Exercise}\n   \n   \\subsection{MANOVA as a linear model}\n   MANOVA (and Hotelling's $T^2$ test) can be considered examples of general linear models.  That is, they have the form:\n   \n   \\begin{equation} \\label{eq:1}\n   \\boldsymbol{Y} = \\boldsymbol{\\beta X} + \\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}\n   \\end{equation}\n   \n   \\noindent where $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ is an $n \\times d$ (i.e., $n$ row and $d$ column) matrix of response variable outcomes,\n   $X$ is either: 1) if explanatory variables are quantitative, an $n \\times a$ matrix, with ones in the first column (representing the intercept) and explanatory variable outcomes in the remaining $a - 1$ columns, or 2) if explanatory variables are categorical, or both quantitative and categorical, a \\textbf{design matrix} whose form will vary with user-prescribed contrasts. $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ will be a $a \\times 1$ vector of parameters, and $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ will be an $n \\times d$ matrix of random error terms. \n   \n   The product $\\boldsymbol{\\beta X}$ in Eq. \\ref{eq:1} represents the \"fit\" part of the linear model, while error (noise) in $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ is represented by the term $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$. All general linear models assume that components of $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ are independent and identically normally distributed.\n   \n      The total sum of squares and cross products matrix $\\boldsymbol{T}$ and the between and within sum of square and cross product matrices $\\boldsymbol{A}$ and $\\boldsymbol{E}$, respectively, can be calculated using linear algebra: \n   \\begin{equation}\n  \\boldsymbol{T} = \\boldsymbol{Y'Y}\n   \\end{equation}\n   \n      \\begin{equation}\n   \\boldsymbol{A} = \\boldsymbol{\\hat{Y}'\\hat{Y}}\n   \\end{equation}\n   \n       \\begin{equation}\n   \\boldsymbol{E} = \\boldsymbol{R'R}\n   \\end{equation}\n   \n   where $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ is a centered response variable matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\hat{Y}}$ is a matrix of fitted values, and $\\boldsymbol{R}$ is a matrix of model residuals.  We define the trace of $\\boldsymbol{T}$ as: \n     \t\t\\[S_T = \\text{tr}(\\boldsymbol{Y'Y}). \\] \n   $S_T$ will contain the sum of the sums of squares for each $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ variable. \n   \n   As always, we estimate $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ in Eq. \\ref{eq:1} with $\\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\beta}}$, using the important equation:\n   \n   \t\t\t\\begin{equation}\n         \\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\beta}}= (\\boldsymbol{X'X})^{-1}\\boldsymbol{X'Y}.\n         \\end{equation}\n   Our matrix of $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ fitted values is also calculated in the conventional way:\n   \\begin{equation}\n          \\boldsymbol{\\hat{Y}}=\\boldsymbol{X\\hat{\\beta}} = \\boldsymbol{HY}. \n   \\end{equation}\n   \n   Where the hat matrix, $\\boldsymbol{H}$, is calculated as:\n   \n   \\begin{equation}\n   \\boldsymbol{H}=\\boldsymbol{X}(\\boldsymbol{X'X})^{-1}\\boldsymbol{X'}.\n   \\end{equation}\n   \n   Thus, the matrix of residuals is:\n   \\[\\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\varepsilon}}=\\boldsymbol{Y}-\\boldsymbol{\\hat{Y}}.\\]\t\t        \t\t\t\n   \n   $\\boldsymbol{T}$  can be partitioned into the matrices $\\boldsymbol{A}$ (i.e., treatment sums of squares) and $\\boldsymbol{E}$  (residual sums of squares).  \n   \\begin{equation}\n   \\boldsymbol{T}=\\boldsymbol{Y'Y}=\\boldsymbol{\\hat{Y}'\\hat{Y}}+\\boldsymbol{R'R} = \\boldsymbol{A} + \\boldsymbol{E}\n   \\end{equation}\n   As a result:  \n   \\begin{equation}\n   \t\tS_T = \\text{tr}(\\boldsymbol{Y'Y}) = \\text{tr}(\\boldsymbol{\\hat{Y}'\\hat{Y}}) +\\text {tr}(\\boldsymbol{R'R})\n       \\end{equation}\t\t\t     \n   \n   As before we have:\n   \\[\n   \\Lambda^*=\\frac{\\boldsymbol{E}}{\\left| \\boldsymbol{A}+\\boldsymbol{E} \\right|} = \\frac{\\left| \\boldsymbol{E} \\right|}{\\left| \\boldsymbol{T} \\right|}\n   \\]\n   \n   \n    \t\t\t\n   \n   \n   \n    \\begin{Exercise}\n    \n    As an application we will use a dataset, popularized by \\citet{jongman1995}, consisting of observations from a Dutch research project in dune meadows on the island of Terschelling from 1973 to 1982 \\citep{batterink1983}.  Along with plant community data from the meadow, several environmental variables were recorded.  These included a variable describing management practices at the sites where community data were gathered.  The management explanatory variable factor had four factor levels: BF = biological farming, HF = hobby farming, NM = nature conservation management, and SF = standard farming. The community data are contained in the dataframes \\texttt{dune} and environmental data, including management are in \\texttt{dune.env} in \\textit{vegan}. For simplicity, we will only consider the first five columns of \\texttt{dune}.\n    \n    First we center the $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ matrix. \n    \n     <<tidy = TRUE>>=\n     library(vegan)\n     data(dune)\n     Y <- as.matrix(apply(dune[,1:5], 2, function(x){scale(x, scale = FALSE, center = TRUE)}))\n     @\n     \n     We know that $\\boldsymbol{T}$ will be \n     \n     <<>>=\n     T <- t(Y) %*% Y\n     T\n     @\n     \n     And $S_T$ is:\n     \n     <<tidy = TRUE>>=\n     ST <- print(sum(diag(T)))\n     @\n     \n     Here is our design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{X}$.\n     \n     <<>>=\n     data(dune.env)\n     attach(dune.env)\n     X <- as.matrix(data.frame(BF = ifelse(Management == \"BF\", 1, 0), \n                               HF = ifelse(Management == \"HF\", 1, 0), \n                               NM = ifelse(Management == \"NM\", 1, 0), \n                               SF = ifelse(Management == \"SF\", 1, 0)))\n     X\n     \n     \n     @\n     \n     For $\\boldsymbol{\\hat{\\beta}}$ we have: \n     \n     <<>>=\n     beta.hat <- solve(t(X) %*% X) %*% t(X) %*% Y\n     beta.hat\n     @\n     \n     Here are our fitted values, $\\boldsymbol{\\hat{Y}}$.  Note that these are just factor level means\n     \n     <<>>=\n     Y.hat <- print(X %*% beta.hat)\n     @\n     \n     Here is our matrix of residuals, $\\boldsymbol{R}$:\n     \n     <<>>=\n     R <- print(Y - Y.hat)\n     @\n     \n     Here is treatment sum of squares and cross product matrix, $\\boldsymbol{A}$.\n     \n     <<>>=\n     A <- t(Y.hat) %*% Y.hat\n     A\n     @\n     \n     And our residual sum of squares and cross product matrix, $\\boldsymbol{E}$.\n     \n     \n     <<>>=\n     E <- t(R) %*% R\n     E\n     @\n     \n     Their sum will equal $\\boldsymbol{T}$.\n     \n     <<>>=\n     T <- A + E\n     T\n     @\n     \n   \n   \n   We have demonstrated that a MANOVA is a linear model just like an ANOVA or a regression.  Let's create a MANOVA in one step and double check our results. \n    \n    <<>>=\n   library(car)\n   red.dune<-as.matrix(dune[,1:5])\n   summary(Manova(lm(red.dune~Management)))\n   @\n   \n   Note that the $\\boldsymbol{A}$ and $\\boldsymbol{E}$ matrices and the value for Wilk's lambda are identical to those we calculated \"by hand\" using linear algebra.\n   \n   \n   \n   \\end{Exercise}\n   \n   \\subsection{Two Way MANOVA}\n   In a two way factorial MANOVA we are comparing the mean vectors of all the different combinations of factor levels from two factors.   Therefore our null and alternative hypotheses for a MANOVA testing factor $A$ (with $a$ levels) and factor $B$ (with $b$ levels) are concerned with both main effects and interactions.\n   \n   For factor $A$ we have the hypotheses:\n   \\begin{center}\n   H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_1} = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_2} = \\dots = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_a}$\n   \\end{center}\\\\\n   \\begin{center}\n   H$_\\text{A}$: At least one $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_{\\ell}}$ is different from the others\n   \\end{center}\\\\\n   \n   For factor $B$ we have the hypotheses:\n   \\begin{center}\n   H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_1} = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_2} = \\dots = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_b}$\n   \\end{center}\\\\\n   \\begin{center}\n   H$_\\text{A}$: At least one $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_{\\mathcal{m}}}$ is different from the others, where $\\mathcal{m} \\in \\{1,2,\\dots, b\\}$.\n   \n   \\end{center}\\\\\n   For the interaction $A:B$ we have the hypotheses:\n   \\begin{center}\n   H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_{11}} = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_{12}} = \\dots = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_{ab}}$\n   \\end{center}\\\\\n   \\begin{center}\n   H$_\\text{A}$: At least one $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_{\\ell\\mathcal{m}}}$ is different from the others.\n   \\end{center}\\\\\n   \n   Two main effects SSCP matrices, along with interaction and error SSCP matrices must be computed.\n   The SSCP matrix for factor A is calculated as:\n   \n   \\begin{equation}\n   \\boldsymbol{A} = \\sum_{\\ell=1}^a bn_0 \\left(\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\ell}-\\bar{X}}\\right)\\left(\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\ell}-\\bar{X}}\\right)' \n   \\end{equation}\n   where $n_0$ is the number of observations in each treatment combination. i.e., the equations shown here require a balanced two way design.\n   \n   The SSCP matrix for factor B is calculated as:\n   \\begin{equation}\n   \\boldsymbol{B} = \\sum_{\\mathcal{m}=1}^b an_0 \\left(\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\mathcal{m}}-\\bar{X}}\\right)\\left(\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\mathcal{m}}-\\bar{X}}\\right)'\n   \\end{equation}\n   \n   The interaction SSCP matrix is calculated as:\n   \\begin{equation}\n   \\boldsymbol{F} = \\sum_{\\ell=1}^a\\sum_{\\mathcal{m}=1}^b  \\left(\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\ell}-\\bar{X}_{\\mathcal{m}}-\\bar{X}_{\\ell\\mathcal{m}} + \\bar{X}}\\right)\\left(\\boldsymbol{\\bar{X}_{\\ell}-\\bar{X}_{\\mathcal{m}}-\\bar{X}_{\\ell\\mathcal{m}} + \\bar{X}}\\right)'\n   \\end{equation}\n   \n   The error SSCP matrix is calculated as:\n   \\begin{equation}\n   \\boldsymbol{E} = \\sum_{\\ell=1}^a\\sum_{\\mathcal{m}=1}^b\\sum_{j=1}^{n_0} \\left(\\boldsymbol{X_{\\ell\\mathcal{m}j}-\\bar{X}_{\\ell\\mathcal{m}}}\\right)\\left(\\boldsymbol{X_{\\ell\\mathcal{m}j}-\\bar{X}_{\\ell\\mathcal{m}}}\\right)'\n   \\end{equation}\n   \n   Table \\ref{tab:MANOVA2way} summarizes the SSCP matrices and their degrees of freedom.\n   \n   {\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.5}\n   \\begin{table}[H]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Two-way MANOVA table.}\n    \\label{tab:MANOVA2way}\n    \\vspace{0.1in}\n    \\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|}\n    \\hline\n   Source of Variation & df                                    & Sums of squares and cross product matrices                                                                                                                                                             \\\\\\hline\n   $A$             & $a - 1$                               & $\\boldsymbol{A}$\n   \\\\\n   $B$             & $b - 1$                               & $\\boldsymbol{B}$\n   \\\\\n   $A:B$            & $(a-1)(b - 1)$                               &   $\\boldsymbol{F}$                      \n   \\\\\n   Error      & $ab(n_0-1)$  &  $\\boldsymbol{E}$ \n   \\\\  \n   Total     & $abn_0-1$     & $\\boldsymbol{A + B + F + E}$  \\\\\n   \\hline\n   \\end{tabular}}\n   \\end{table}\n   \n   \\begin{Exercise}\n   In Exercise 1 we considered data describing the effect of substrate on Yellowstone NP cliff communities with respect to two substrate types.  \\citet{aho2006} also recorded an additional categorical variable: water status, with levels dry, mesic, or hygric.  The water designations were based on distances of waterfalls which drained the cliff faces.  We will simultaneously examine the effect of substrate and water status.  We have three sets of hypotheses.\n   \n   For substrate (Lime and Andesite) we have:\n   \\begin{center} H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_L} = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_C}$\n   \\end{center}\n   \\begin{center} H$_\\text{A}$: $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_L} \\neq \\boldsymbol{\\mu_C}$\n   \\end{center}\n   \n   For water level (Dry, Mesic, Hygric) we have:\n   \\begin{center} H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_H} = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_M} = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_X}$\n   \\end{center}\n   \\begin{center} H$_\\text{A}$: At least one $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_{\\mathcal{m}}}}$ is different from the others.\n   \\end{center}\n   \n   For the interaction we have the hypotheses:\n   \\begin{center} H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_{LH}} = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_{LM}} = \\dots = \\boldsymbol{\\mu_{ab}}$\n   \\end{center}\n   \\begin{center} H$_\\text{A}$: At least one $\\boldsymbol{\\mu_{\\ell\\mathcal{m}}}$ is different from the others.\n   \\end{center}\n   \n   <<>>=\n   cliff.env <- data.frame(cliff.env)\n   cliff.lm <- lm(as.matrix(cliff.sp) ~ sub * water, data = cliff.env)\n   summary(manova(cliff.lm))\n   @\n   \n   Our $P$-values for substrate and water are both significant.  Unfortunately, so is their interaction, indicating non-additivity of the factors.  This will interfere with interpretation of main effects. MANOVA analogues of many different experimental designs (e.g. split plot, nested designs) techniques have been developed.  Some of these are described in \\citet{hand1987}.\n   \\end{Exercise}\n   \n   \\subsection{Multivariate General Linear Model Assumptions} \n   Techniques described here have assumptions essentially identical to those for univariate general linear models.  In particular we assume that:\n   \\begin{enumerate}\n   \\item Observations are independent, \n   \\item Population covariance matrices for the treatments are all equal. \n   \\item The dependent variables have a multivariate normal distribution.  \n   \\end{enumerate}\n   Unfortunately, these assumptions are notoriously difficult to test.  Diagnostic plots will generally poorly express the multivariate character of the $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ variables, particularly when the number of response variables is greater than two.  In addition, formal hypothesis tests for multivariate normality and homoscedasticity tend to be extremely stringent.  According to \\citet{venables2013} \n   \\begin{quote}\n   \"\\dots the tests are so sensitive to multivariate normality as to be almost no practical use\" \\dots in addition \\dots \"the departure from the homogeneity assumptions hurts you before statistical significance is reached, and it is better to act as if the (assumption of) homoscedasticity does not hold.\"   \n   \\end{quote}\n   \n   We will examine multivariate assumptions using the \\texttt{cliff.sp} dataset.\n   \n   %\\begin{enumerate}\n   \\subsubsection{Observations are independent}\n   \n   \n   In all conventional linear models we assume that observations are independent.  We can attempt to verify this in multivariate data by examining the independence of the pairs of $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ variables. \\textbf{Chi-plots} \\citep{fisher1985, fisher2001} allow visual diagnosis of bivariate independence (two $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ variables at a time).   In a chi-plot pairwise differences within variables are converted into the variables $\\lambda$ and $\\chi$.  If a large number of observations fall beyond the range $\\pm 1.78 \\lambda/(n)0.5$ then there is strong evidence for a lack of non-independence.  Details on chi-plot construction are given in the documentation for the function \\texttt{chi.plot} in \\textit{asbio} and in \\citet[pp. 24-25][]{everitt2006}.  \n   \n   Figure \\ref{fig:chi} shows chi-plots for a number of species with respect to the lichen \\textit{Staurothele drummondii} (species 5).  There are clearly problems with our assumption of independence here.\n   \n   \\begin{figure}[H]\n   <<fig.width=4.75, fig.height =4.75, tidy = TRUE>>=\n   library(asbio)\n   par(mfrow=c(3,3),mar=c(4,4,0.5,0.5))\n   chi.plot(cliff.sp[,5],cliff.sp[,1])\n   chi.plot(cliff.sp[,5],cliff.sp[,2])\n   chi.plot(cliff.sp[,5],cliff.sp[,3])\n   chi.plot(cliff.sp[,5],cliff.sp[,4])\n   chi.plot(cliff.sp[,5],cliff.sp[,6])\n   chi.plot(cliff.sp[,5],cliff.sp[,7])\n   chi.plot(cliff.sp[,5],cliff.sp[,8])\n   chi.plot(cliff.sp[,5],cliff.sp[,9])\n   chi.plot(cliff.sp[,5],cliff.sp[,10]) \n   @\n   \\caption{Chi plots for 6 species with respect to the lichen \\textit{Staurothele drummondii}.}\n   \\label{fig:chi}\n   \\end{figure}\n   \n   %\\item Observations follow a multivariate normal distribution\n   \\subsubsection{Observations follow a multivariate normal distribution}\n   \n   This assumption is difficult to verify.  Hypothesis tests tend to be too stringent, and multivariate diagnostic plots only allow viewing of two variables at a time.  Univariate normality of course can be verified using normal probability plots.  However while marginal non-normality indicates multivariate non-normality, marginal normality (while a requirement for multivariate normality) does not insure that $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ variables collectively follow a multivariate normal distribution.  \n   \n   One way to visually check for bivariate normality is to use \\textbf{bivariate boxplots} \\citep{goldberg1992}.  Bivariate boxplots will contain a pair of concentric ellipses known as the \"hinge\" and the \"fence\".  The hinge ellipse will contain 50\\% of the data.  The outer \"fence\" ellipse is generally at a distance that corresponds to a 99\\% confidence bound on a single experimental unit \\citep{everitt2006}.  Observations outside of the fence constitute outliers from multivariate normality. Estimates of location, scale, and correlation are required for the plots, and these values robust biweight $M$-estimators are recommended for creation of the plots \\citep{goldberg1992}.  This is the default procedure in \\texttt{bv.boxplot} in \\textit{asbio}.  Computations for the robust estimators in \\texttt{bv.boxplot} are currently handled by the function \\texttt{biweight} from \\citet{everitt2006}.   In Fig. \\ref{fig:bv} we create bivariate boxplots for a number of the $(11^2 - 11)/2 = 55$ pairwise species combinations.  There are serious problems with non-normality.  Most of the points exceed the fence to such a large degree that the ellipse no longer even visible.\n   \n   \\begin{figure}[H]\n   <<fig.width=6, fig.height =6, tidy = TRUE>>=\n   par(mfrow=c(3,3),mar=c(4,4,0.5,0.5))\n   bv.boxplot(cliff.sp[,5],cliff.sp[,1],xlab=\"Y5\",ylab=\"Y1\")\n   bv.boxplot(cliff.sp[,4],cliff.sp[,1],xlab=\"Y4\",ylab=\"Y1\")\n   bv.boxplot(cliff.sp[,1],cliff.sp[,6],xlab=\"Y1\",ylab=\"Y6\")\n   bv.boxplot(cliff.sp[,5],cliff.sp[,4],xlab=\"Y5\",ylab=\"Y4\")\n   bv.boxplot(cliff.sp[,5],cliff.sp[,6],xlab=\"Y5\",ylab=\"Y6\")\n   bv.boxplot(cliff.sp[,4],cliff.sp[,6],xlab=\"Y4\",ylab=\"Y6\")\n   bv.boxplot(cliff.sp[,1],cliff.sp[,9],xlab=\"Y1\",ylab=\"Y9\")\n   bv.boxplot(cliff.sp[,4],cliff.sp[,9],xlab=\"Y4\",ylab=\"Y9\")\n   bv.boxplot(cliff.sp[,5],cliff.sp[,9],xlab=\"Y5\",ylab=\"Y9\") \n   @\n   \\caption{Bivariate boxplots comparing the bivariate distributions of $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ variables to a bivariate normal distribution.  All of the plots indicate a serious departure from bivariate normality.}\n   \\label{fig:bv}\n   \\end{figure}\n   \n   \\subsubsection{Covariance matrices for treatments are equal}\n   \n   A particularly difficult assumption to test is that of multivariate homoscedasticity.  A number of methods have been proposed for this purpose including the \\textbf{Kullback Test} \n   (Kullback 1959) which has the null hypothesis:\n   \\begin{center}\n   H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\Sigma_1} = \\boldsymbol{\\Sigma_2}= \\dots= \\boldsymbol{\\Sigma_{a}}$\n   \\end{center}\\\\\n   where $a$ = the number of groups (e.g., factor levels) in $\\boldsymbol{X}$.  The Kullback test statistic is calculated as:   \n   \n   \\begin{equation}\n   {{X}^{2}}^*=\\sum\\limits_{\\ell=1}^{a}{\\frac{\\left({{n}_{\\ell}}-1 \\right)}{2}\\ln }\\frac{\\left| S \\right|}{\\left| {{S}_{\\ell}} \\right|}\n   \\end{equation}\n   \n   where $|\\boldsumbol{S}|$ is the determinant of a covariance matrix that includes all data in $\\boldsymbol{Y}$, and $|\\boldsumbol{S_{\\ell}}|$ is the determinant of the $\\ell$th variance/covariance matrix. If H$_0$ is true then the test statistic, $X^2$*, follows a chi-squared distribution with $(a - 1)d(d + 1)/2$ degrees of freedom.  This diagnostic test can be run using the function \\texttt{Kullback} in \\textit{asbio}, although it will have difficulties with datasets with frequent zeroes.  This is because determinants for covariance matrices will be zero, leading to an undefined test statistic.  \n   \n   <<>>=\n   Y1 <- rnorm(100, 10, 2)\n   Y2 <- rnorm(100, 15, 2)\n   Y <- cbind(Y1, Y2)\n   X <- factor(c(rep(1, 50), rep(2, 50)))\n   Kullback(Y, X)\n   @\n   \n   \\section{Alternatives to Multivariate General Linear Models}\n   There are a number of robust alternatives to multivariate linear models that allow inferential tests.  This is important given the difficulty of testing multivariate linear models assumptions, and the sensitivity of their diagnostic tests.  For many of these tests, selection of an appropriate resemblance measure is of great importance.  Although Maronna (2006) discusses some useful developments, $M$-estimators are problematic for multivariate hypothesis testing \\citep{wilcox2012}, and applications for these procedures are not pursued here.  \n   \n   \\subsection{MRPP}\n   An approach called \\textbf{Multiple Response Permutation Procedure} (\\textbf{MRPP}) provides a robust alternative for a one-way MANOVA.  Given  factor levels $\\ell \\in  \\{1,2, \\dots, a\\}$, our hypotheses are:\n   \n \\begin{center} H$_0$: The multivariate populations are equal for all $a$ factor levels.\n \\end{center}\n \\begin{center} H$_\\text{A}$: At least one multivariate population is not equal to the others. \n \\end{center}\n \n In a community ecology this will generally equate to a null hypothesis of no community difference among factor levels.  A good introduction to MRPP can be found in \\citet{biondini1988}, and an effective ecological perspective to MRPP is provided in \\citet{mccune2002}.\\\\\n \n The MRPP process has five steps.  \n \\begin{enumerate}\n \\item Calculate a distance matrix \\boldsymbol{D}.  Various distance measures can be used.  Euclidean distances and squared Euclidean distances were utilized in the early literature of MRPP although dissimilarities like the Steinhaus (Bray-Curtis) index are being increasingly used by ecologists and other biologists.  If MRPP is to be used in conjunction with an ordination, then the same distance algorithm should be used with both analyses \\citep{mccune2002}.  \n\\item Calculate the average distance $d_{\\ell}$ of items to each other, within each of the $a$ groups, $\\ell \\in  \\{1,2, \\dots, a\\}$.\n\\item Calculate the weighted mean within group distance,  $\\delta$.\n\\begin{equation}\n\\delta =\\sum\\limits_{\\ell =1}^{a}{{{w}_{\\ell }}{{d}_{\\ell }}}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $w_{\\ell}$ is the weight given to the $\\ell$th group.  The weighting of groups will depend on the number of items in a group.  Three different kinds of weights are generally used (Table \\ref{table:MRPP}).\n\n\n{\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.5}\n\\begin{table}[H]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Methods for weighting groups with MRPP.}\n\\label{table:MRPP}\n\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n\\hline\n\\multicolumn{1}{c}{Weight equation} & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{Comment} \n\\\\\n\\hline\n${{w}_{\\ell}}=\\frac{{{n}_{j}}}{\\sum\\limits_{\\ell =1}^{a}{{{n}_{\\ell }}}}=\\frac{{{n}_{\\ell}}}{n}$ & \\begin{tabular}[c]{@{}l@{}}Default in PC-ORD and \\texttt{mrpp} in \\textit{vegan}.\\end{tabular}                                                                              \\\\\n${{w}_{\\ell }}=\\frac{{{n}_{\\ell }}-1}{\\sum\\limits_{\\ell =1}^{a}{\\left( {{n}_{\\ell }}-1 \\right)}}=\\frac{{{n}_{\\ell }}-1}{n-1}$                                                        & \\begin{tabular}[c]{@{}l@{}}With squared Euclidean distances, equates \\\\ to a 2 sample $t$-test (for two groups), \\\\ or a one-way ANOVA $F$-test (for $> 2$ groups).\\end{tabular} \\\\\n${{w}_{\\ell }}=\\frac{{{n}_{\\ell }}\\left( {{n}_{\\ell }}-1 \\right)/2}{\\sum\\limits_{j=1}^{g}{{{n}_{\\ell }}({{n}_{\\ell }}-1)/2}}=\\frac{{{n}_{\\ell }}\\left( {{n}_{\\ell }}-1 \\right)/2}{n\\left( n-1 \\right)/2}$                                     &    \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{tabular}}\n\\end{table}\n\\item Using permutation procedures, determine probability of observing an as small or smaller than the one derived from the original data.  This is done by permuting the pairwise distances in the distance matrix and finding the number of times permutation $\\delta$'s are less than the observed  $\\delta$.  We divide this count by the number of permutations to get an empirical $P$-value.\n\n\n\\item Calculate the \\textbf{chance corrected within group agreement}, $A$.  $A$ \\citep{mccune2002} provides a measure of within group homogeneity compared to random expectation. \n\\begin{equation}\nA=1-\\frac{\\delta }{{{{\\bar{X}}}_{\\delta }}}\n\\end{equation}\nWhen all items are identical within groups, then $\\delta = 0$, and $A = 1$ (the highest possible value for $A$).  If heterogeneity equals the expectation by chance then $A = 0$.  Finally if there is less homogeneity within groups then expected by chance then $A < 0$.  In community ecology values for $A$ are commonly below $0.1$ even when observed  $\\delta$'s are significantly different from the expected $\\delta$'s under H$_0$.  Values greater than 0.3 are exceedingly rare \\citep{mccune2002}.  \n\\end{enumerate} \n\n% \\subsubsection{MRPP in \\textbf{R}}\nMRPP can be run in R using the function \\texttt{mrpp} from the library \\textit{vegan}.  \n\n% In addition, a wrapper for \\texttt{mrpp} called \\texttt{Mrpp} exists in the \\extit{plant.ecol} package which provides additional output including alculation of the test statistic:\n% \n% \\begin{equation}\n% T=(\\delta -{{\\bar{X}}_{\\delta }})/{{S}_{\\delta }}\n% \\end{equation}\n% \n% ${{\\bar{X}}_{\\delta }}$ and ${{S}_{\\delta }}$ are the mean and standard deviation of the weighted intra-group dissimilarities given permutations of the data.  $T$ describes the separation between the groups.  The more negative $T$ is, the stronger the separation.  Conversely, positive $T$'s obviously indicate exactly the opposite effect of the one we are trying to show in the alternative hypothesis.  If H$_0$ is true, then $T$ will follows a \\textbf{Pearson type III distribution}.  The Pearson type III distribution can be defined with three parameters $\\mu$ (the expected value) $\\sigma$, the standard deviation, and $\\gamma$, a shape parameter.  To define the appropriate null distribution in MRPP these values are estimated using a permutation distribution of $T$'s.\n\n\\begin{Exercise}\nTo demonstrate we will use the \\texttt{dune} community vegetation dataset. \n\n<<comment = NA>>=\ndata(dune)\ndata(dune.env)\nmrpp(dune, dune.env$Management)\n@\n\n\\end{Exercise}\n\n\n\\subsection{Mantel tests}\nMantel tests \\citep{mantel1967} can be used to compare two resemblance matrices describing the same sites or species.  For instance we may have species composition data for a set of sites and environmental data for the same sites.  If the environmental variables are controlling species distribution and composition we would expect distances between sites in species space to be similar to distances between sites in environmental space\n\nIn the Mantel test the element-wise correlation between distances in the two matrices is determined.  This will be the standardized Mantel test statistic.  Our hypotheses \\citet{legendre2012} are:\n\nH$_0$: The true multivariate patterns underlying $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ are not linearly (or monotonically, depending on the correlation estimator) associated with a second matrix $\\boldsymbol{X}$ which describes a different group of measurements on the same experimental units as those in $\\boldsymbol{Y}$.\\\\\n\\indent H$_\\text{A}$: There is a linear (or monotonic) association between populations represented by $\\boldsymbol{X}$ and $\\boldsymbol{Y}$.   \n\nIn the R function mantel from vegan the rows and columns of the first specified dissimilarity matrix are permuted and the correlation is rechecked.  The number of times that the permuted correlations are greater than the observed correlation, divided by the number of permutations, provides an empirical $P$-value. Any correlation metric from the function \\texttt{cor} can be used to assess the correlation between the two distance matrices; i.e. Pearson product moment , along with non-parametric Spearman or Kendall correlation procedures.  Pearson's correlation is the default.  \n\n\\begin{Exercise}\nTo demonstrate Mantel testing  we  will  use  two  datasets  from  the library \\textit{vegan}  describing Scandinavian taiga/tundra.  Vegetation data are contained in the dataset \\texttt{varespec} and soil chemistry data for the same sites are contained in the dataset \\texttt{varechem}. \n\n<<>>=\ndata(varespec)\ndata(varechem)\nveg.dist <- vegdist(varespec)\nenv.dist <- vegdist(scale(varechem),\"euclid\")\nmantel(veg.dist, env.dist)\n@\n\n\\end{Exercise}\n\n\\subsubsection{Mantel tests on more than two matrices}\nBiological researchers may often confront situations where more than two groups of variables need to be compared resulting in more than two resemblance matrices.  For instance, these variables might include 1) soil chemistry, 2) community composition, and 3) climate descriptors measured for the same sites (i.e., experimental units).   Consider three resemblance matrices $\\boldsymbol{A}$, $\\boldsymbol{B}$, and $\\boldsymbol{C}$.  \\citet{smouse1986} proposed that $\\boldsymbol{A}$ be permuted while holding $\\boldsymbol{B}$ and $\\boldsymbol{C}$ constant.  This allows tests based on calculation of the partial correlation coefficients. \n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\n\\item  We first calculate $R_{AB}$, $R_{AC}$, $R_{BC}$, and the partial correlation of $\\boldsymbol{A}$ to $\\boldsymbol{B}$ holding $\\boldsymbol{C}$ constant at its mean, $R_{AB|C}$.  This is the test statistic  \n\n\\begin{equation}\nR_{AB|C} = \\frac{{{R}_{AB}}-{{R}_{AC}}{{R}_{BC}}}{\\sqrt{1-R_{13}^{2}}\\sqrt{1-R_{23}^{2}}}.\n\\end{equation}\n\n\\item We then permute $\\boldsymbol{A}$, giving $\\boldsymbol{A^*}$, while holding $\\boldsymbol{B}$ and $\\boldsymbol{C}$ constant and calculate $R_{A^*B|C}$.  We repeat this process a large number of times, and create sampling distribution $R_{A^*B|C}$ values to compare to the initial estimate $R_{AB|C}$.  This allows computation of an empirical $P$-value, i.e., the number of $R_{A^*B|C}$ outcomes more extreme than $R_{AB|C}$ divided by the number of permutations.  The function \\texttt{mantel.partial} from \\textit{vegan} provides partial Mantel tests. \n\n\\end{enumerate}\nInterpreting partial Mantel tests can be difficult though details are given in \\citep[][p 170]{legendre2012}.  Partial Mantel tests are often used in comparing a matrix containing community data $\\boldsymbol{A}$, to a matrix of environmental variables $\\boldsymbol{B}$, to a matrix of spatial coordinates $\\boldsymbol{C}$.  \\citet{oden1992} showed that using this method allows an analysis resistant to the effects of spatial autocorrelation. \n\n\\subsection{PERMANOVA}\nThe approach described below has been coined Permutational Multivariate Analysis of Variance or PERMANOVA \\citep{anderson2001, mcardle2001}.  The method allows multivariate considerations of main effects,  interactions, nestedness, and even random effects. As with a one way MANOVA, a one way PERMANOVA is concerned with the hypotheses:\n\n \\begin{center} H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\tau_1} = \\boldsymbol{\\tau_2} = \\dots = \\boldsymbol{\\tau_a} = \\boldsymbol{0}$\n \\end{center}\n \\begin{center} H$_\\text{A}$: At least one $\\boldsymbol{\\tau_{\\ell}} \\neq \\boldsymbol{0}$ \n \\end{center}\n where $\\boldsymbol{\\tau_{\\ell}}$ is the vector of effect sizes for the ${\\ell}$th factor level.\n\nThe PERMANOVA method is possible because the sums of squares for any multivariate linear model (i.e., MANOVA) can be calculated from a Euclidean distance matrix of the $n \\times p$, $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ dataset \\citep{mcardle2001}, where $n$ is the number of experimental units and $p$ is the number of explanatory variables. \n\nFor a centered $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ matrix (i.e. matrix elements are column means in $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ are subtracted from their respective column elements) relevant information describing $\\boldsymbol{Y}$ can be found in the $\\boldsymbol{Y'Y}$ outer product which has dimensions $n \\times n$.  Furthermore, and more importantly from the perspective of PERMANOVA, an outer product matrix can be created from \\emph{any} $n \\times n$ symmetric distance or dissimilarity matrix.\n\nAs we know the matrix $\\boldsymbol{T}$  in MANOVA can be partitioned into the matrices $\\boldsymbol{A}$ (i.e., treatment sums of squares) and $\\boldsymbol{E}$  (residual sums of squares).  \n\\[\n\\boldsymbol{T}=\\boldsymbol{Y'Y}=\\boldsymbol{\\hat{Y}'\\hat{Y}}+\\boldsymbol{R'R} = \\boldsymbol{A} + \\boldsymbol{E}\n\\]\nAs a result:  \n\\[\n\t\tS_T = \\text{tr}(\\boldsymbol{Y'Y}) = \\text{tr}(\\boldsymbol{\\hat{Y}'\\hat{Y}}) +\\text {tr}(\\boldsymbol{R'R})\n\\]     \nA suitable pseudo $F$-statistic \\citet{mcardle2001} is:\n \t\t\\begin{equation}\n     F^* = \\frac{\\text{tr}(\\boldsymbol{\\hat{Y}'\\hat{Y}})}{a-1}/\\frac{\\text{tr}(\\boldsymbol{R'R})}{n- a} \n     \\end{equation}\n     \nThis test statistic can be used in permutation testing to find $P = P(F \\ge F^*)$, where $F$ is a permutation distribution of empirically derived $F^*$ statistics.  The degrees of freedom can, of course, be dropped for the permutation analysis.\n\nThe product $\\boldsymbol{Y'Y}$ will be a $p \\times p$ matrix while $\\boldsymbol{YY'}$ will be a $n \\times n$ matrix. However\n$\\text{tr}(\\boldsymbol{Y'Y})$ will equal $\\text{tr}(\\boldsymbol{YY'})$.  As a result the pseudo-$F$ test statistic can be calculated using the inner $\\boldsymbol{Y'Y}$ or outer $\\boldsymbol{YY'}$ product matrices, i.e,\n \\begin{equation}\n   \t{S_T} = \\text{tr}(\\boldsymbol{YY'}) = \\text{tr}(\\boldsymbol{\\hat{Y}'\\hat{Y}}) +\\text{tr}(\\boldsymbol{RR'}).\n     \\end{equation}\n \nThis is important because an outer product matrix can be obtained from any $n \\times n$ dissimilarity or distance matrix using the method of \\citet{gower1966} described below.  Let $\\boldsymbol{D}$ be an $n \\times n$ distance or dissimilarity matrix with elements $d_{ij}$.  Let $\\boldsymbol{Z} = -1/2 (d_{ij})^2$.  The Gower centered matrix, $\\boldsymbol{G}$ is:\n \t\t\t\n\\begin{equation}\n\\boldsymbol{G} = (\\boldsymbol{I} -1/n \\boldsymbol{11'}) \\boldsymbol{Z}(\\boldsymbol{I} -1/n \\boldsymbol{11'})\t\t\t\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\boldsymbol{I}$ is an $n \\times n$ identity matrix and $\\boldsymbol{1}$ is a $1 \\times n$ column of ones.  We can partition $\\boldsymbol{G}$ the same way we partitioned $\\boldsymbol{S_T}$ above.  Our pseudo-$F$ test statistic is:\n\\begin{equation}\n    F^* = \\frac{\\text{tr}(\\boldsymbol{HGH)}}{a-1}/\\frac{\\text{tr}\\{(\\boldsymbol{I-H})\\boldsymbol{G}(\\boldsymbol{I}-\\boldsymbol{H})\\}}{n-a} \n\\end{equation} \nAgain, the $P$-value can be calculated using permutation procedures.  \n\n \\begin{Exercise}\n A MANOVA implicitly uses Euclidean distance.  To demonstrate this we will try to get the same result we did in Exercise 3 using a Euclidean distance matrix rather than the raw \\texttt{dune} data.  Here is an $n \\times n$ Euclidean distance matrix for the reduced \\texttt{dune} dataset.   \n \n <<>>=\n D <- as.matrix(vegdist(dune[,1:5],\"euclidean\"))\n @\n \n Next we create the Gower centered matrix, $\\bm{G}$.\n \n <<>>=\n Z <- -.5*(D^2)\n I <- matrix(0,20,20)\n diag(I) <- 1\n one <- as.matrix(rep(1,20))\n \n G <- (I - ((1/20) * (one %*% t(one)))) %*% Z %*% (I - ((1/20) * (one %*% t(one))))\n @\n \n Our hat matrix is:\n \n <<>>=\n X <- with(dune.env, as.matrix(data.frame(BF = ifelse(Management == \"BF\", 1, 0), \n                               HF = ifelse(Management == \"HF\", 1, 0), \n                               NM = ifelse(Management == \"NM\", 1, 0), \n                               SF = ifelse(Management == \"SF\", 1, 0))))\n H <- X %*% solve(t(X) %*% X) %*% t(X)\n @\n\n Thus the treatment sum of squares is:\n\n<<>>=\nsum(diag(H%*%G%*%H))\n@\n\nand the residual sum of squares is:\n\n<<>>=\nsum(diag((I-H)%*%G%*%(I-H)))\n@\n\nOur pseudo-$F$ test statistic based on $\\boldsymbol{A}$ and $\\boldsymbol{E}$ matrices is:\n\n<<>>=\nY <- as.matrix(apply(dune[,1:5], 2, function(x){scale(x, scale = FALSE, \n                                                      center = TRUE)}))\nbeta.hat <- solve(t(X) %*% X) %*% t(X) %*% Y\nA <- t(Y.hat) %*% Y.hat\nR <- Y - Y.hat\nE <- t(R) %*% R\nF<-(sum(diag(A))/(4-1))/ (sum(diag(E))/(20-4))\nF\n@\n\n\nCalculating the pseudo-$F$ test statistic using the community distance matrix we have:\n \n <<>>=\n F <- (sum(diag(H %*% G %*% H))/(4 - 1))/\n  (sum(diag((I - H) %*% G %*% (I - H)))/(20 - 4))\n F\n @\n \n This pseudo $F$-statistics are identical.  The lessons we have learned here are: 1) we can run a MANOVA-type analysis using a distance matrix, and 2) using this approach with a Euclidean distance matrix provides us with the same result as a MANOVA.  It follows that this approach will extend to any resemblance matrix.  This may be of particular interest with ecological data where Euclidean distances are generally inappropriate.  \n \\end{Exercise}\n\n\\begin{Exercise}\nA PERMANOVA analysis can be obtained in R with the function \\texttt{adonis} from library \\textit{vegan}.  In the one way PERMANOVA of the \\texttt{dune} data, we are interested in the hypotheses:\n\n \\begin{center} H$_0$: $\\boldsymbol{\\tau_1} = \\boldsymbol{\\tau_2} = \\dots = \\boldsymbol{\\tau_3} = \\boldsymbol{\\tau_4} = \\boldsymbol{0}$\n \\end{center}\n \\begin{center} H$_\\text{A}$: At least one $\\boldsymbol{\\tau_{\\ell}} \\neq \\boldsymbol{0}$ \n \\end{center}\n \n <<idy = TRUE>>=\nadonis(dune[,1:5] ~ dune.env$Management, method = \"euclidean\", permutation = 1000)\n@\n\n Note key components in the R output, i.e. the sums of squares and the test statistic which are identical to our hand-calculated results.\n\\end{Exercise}\n\n\\subsection{Assumptions for alternatives to multivariate general linear models}\nThe following assumptions should be considered when applying PERMANOVA and other resemblance measure based permutation techniques discussed here (i.e., MRPP, Mantel tests).\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item The resemblance measure should adequately represent the variation in the data.\n\\item The sample units are independent.  The usual problems with pseudoreplication and repeated measures are the same as with linear models.\n\\item\tThe relative weighting of variables has been controlled prior to calculating the distance measure such that the weighting of variables is appropriate to answering the biological/ecological questions of interest.  This may require standardization of explanatory or response variables.  \n\\end{enumerate}\n\n   \\section{Final Thoughts}\n   Multivariate hypothesis tests (whether they are based on linear models or robust analogs) do little more than provide a test statistic and a $P$-value.  Actually describing what these multivariate (e.g., community-level) differences are can be done in a number of ways.  For instance we can: \n   \\begin{enumerate}\n   \\item Use ordination to visualize the multivariate similarity of experimental units in response space (or \\textit{vice versa}).\n   \\item Perform cluster analyses to objectively relationships of multivariate groups, including hierarchical structures. \n   \\item Identify indicator responses (e.g., species) separating groups of interest. Alternatives include indicator species analysis \\citep{dufrene1997} or Murdoch indicator scores \\citep{aho2006}.\n   \\item Use discriminant analysis to identify which variables were most effective in predicting membership of sites to groups, e.g., Discriminant Function Analysis (DFA) and Classification and Regression Tree analysis (CART).   \n   \\end{enumerate}\n\n\\section{Exercises}\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\citet{john} introduced one way MANOVA by considering a dataset describing the effect of ownership (private party, non-profit, or governmental) on nursing care costs in Wisconsin.  Four types of costs were computed: $Y_1$ = cost of nursing labor, $Y_2$ = cost of dietary labor, $Y_3$ = cost of plant operation and maintenance labor, and $Y_4$ = cost of housekeeping and laundry labor.  Responses were measured in hours per patient day. \n    \\begin{center}\n    {\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.5}\n    \\begin{tabular}{ccc}\n    \\hline\n    Factor level & Number of obs. & Sample mean vectors \\\\\n    \\hline\n    $\\ell = 1$ (private)             &              $n_1 = 271$                   &    $\\boldsymbol{\\bar{x}_{1}}=\\left[ \\begin{matrix} \n       2.066 \\\\ \n       0.480  \\\\\n       0.082  \\\\\n       0.360  \\\\\n    \\end{matrix} \\right]$ \\\\\n    $\\ell = 2$ (non-profit)           &              $n_2 = 138$                &     $\\boldsymbol{\\bar{x}_{2}}=\\left[ \\begin{matrix}\n       2.167  \\\\\n       0.596  \\\\\n       0.124  \\\\\n       0.418  \\\\\n    \\end{matrix} \\right]$ \\\\\n    \\ell = 3$ (government)           &              $n_3 = 107$                &    $\\boldsymbol{\\bar{x}_{3}}=\\left[ \\begin{matrix}\n       2.273  \\\\\n       0.521  \\\\\n       0.125  \\\\\n       0.383  \\\\\n    \\end{matrix} \\right]$ \\\\\n    \\hline\n    \\end{tabular}}\n    \\end{center}\n    \n    \\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item What would be the appropriate multivariate hypotheses?\n    \\item Calculate the $\\boldsymbol{A}$ matrix\n    \\item Calculate $\\boldsymbol{T}$, let \n    \\[\\boldsymbol{E} = \\left[ \\begin{matrix}\n    182.962 & {} & {} & {}  \\\\\n    4.408 & 8.2 & {} & {}  \\\\\n    1.695 & 0.633 & 1.484 & {}  \\\\\n    9.581 & 2.428 & 0.394 & 6.538  \\\\\n    \\end{matrix} \\right]\\]\n    \\item\tCalculate Wilk's lambda.\n    \\item\tCalculate the correct test statistic given that $p = 4$ and $a = 3$.\n    \\item\tCalculate the $P$-value.  State your conclusions.\n    \\end{enumerate}\n\n\n\\item A wildlife ecologist is wondering if the environment of Absaroka bighorn sheep differs from those of Sierra Nevada bighorn sheep (i.e., is their $n$-dimensional hyperniche the same).  Using a large sampling team determines the average slope, aspect, and \\% vegetation cover, for the environment of 10 Absaroka herds and 10 Sierra Nevada herds.  \n\n\\begin{center}\n{\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1}\n\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n\\hline\nHerd Number & Location & \\% Cover & Slope (Degrees) & Aspect (degrees from N) \\\\\n\\hline\n1           & A        & 40       & 21              & 45                      \\\\\n2           & A        & 45       & 19              & 76                      \\\\\n3           & A        & 60       & 24              & 43                      \\\\\n4           & A        & 56       & 22              & 56                      \\\\\n5           & A        & 72       & 26              & 78                      \\\\\n6           & A        & 65       & 24              & 72                      \\\\\n7           & A        & 71       & 27              & 19                      \\\\\n8           & A        & 66       & 21              & 26                      \\\\\n9           & A        & 49       & 23              & 29                      \\\\\n10          & A        & 67       & 24              & 54                      \\\\\n11          & S        & 34       & 19              & 132                     \\\\\n12          & S        & 39       & 17              & 105                     \\\\\n13          & S        & 38       & 16              & 115                     \\\\\n14          & S        & 45       & 15              & 71                      \\\\\n15          & S        & 44       & 17              & 101                     \\\\\n16          & S        & 41       & 18              & 103                     \\\\\n17          & S        & 46       & 19              & 89                      \\\\\n18          & S        & 49       & 23              & 99                      \\\\\n19          & S        & 54       & 24              & 100                     \\\\\n20          & S        & 59       & 18              & 154                    \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{tabular}}\n\\end{center}\n\n    \\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item\tWrite this data into Excel and import it into R.\n    \\item\tWhat would be appropriate hypotheses?\n    \\item\tIs it appropriate to standardize the data?  Why or why not?\n    \\item\tRun an MRPP analysis using Euclidean distance.\n    \\item\tRun the hypotheses with an MRPP analysis using Bray-Curtis dissimilarity.\n    \\item\tInterpret the results from c), including the $A$-statistic, and observed and expected delta.\n    \\end{enumerate}\n\n\\item An ecologist is wondering if riparian plant communities differ with respect to four plants species of particular interest.  Two locations (A and B) were tested for change in percent cover of the species after application of three treatments (1 = control, 2 = exclosure from elk, 3 = exclosure from elk and nutrient supplement).     \n\n\\begin{center}\n{\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1}\n\\begin{tabular}{ccccccc}\n\\hline\nPlot Number & Location & Management & Sp 1 & Sp. 2 & Sp 3 & Sp 4 \\\\\n\\hline\n1           & A        & 1          & -3   & 3     & 5    & 0    \\\\\n2           & A        & 1          & 0    & -3    & 0    & 2    \\\\\n3           & B        & 1          & 2    & 0     & 0    & -3   \\\\\n4           & B        & 1          & -2   & 0     & -1   & 1    \\\\\n5           & A        & 2          & 23   & 8     & 4    & 12   \\\\\n6           & A        & 2          & 13   & 3     & 3    & 13   \\\\\n7           & A        & 2          & 3    & 6     & 2    & 17   \\\\\n8           & B        & 2          & 12   & 10    & 7    & 21   \\\\\n9           & B        & 2          & 10   & 4     & 6    & 22   \\\\\n10          & B        & 2          & 8    & 7     & 5    & 25   \\\\\n11          & A        & 3          & 22   & 12    & 10   & 16   \\\\\n12          & A        & 3          & 29   & 13    & 3    & 14   \\\\\n13          & B        & 3          & 18   & 8     & 9    & 12   \\\\\n14          & A        & 3          & 15   & 14    & 6    & 16   \\\\\n15          & A        & 3          & 4    & 8     & 12   & 37   \\\\\n16          & A        & 3          & 22   & 22    & 8    & 22   \\\\\n17          & A        & 1          & 6    & -2    & -2   & 2    \\\\\n18          & A        & 2          & 9    & 9     & 12   & 25   \\\\\n19          & B        & 3          & 14   & 9     & 13   & 35   \\\\\n20          & B        & 1          & -5   & 2     & 0    & -1  \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{tabular}}\n\\end{center}\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Write this data into Excel and import it into R.\n\\item\tWhat are the hypotheses of interest?  Note that you will have a hypothesis for each $P$-value that is calculated for you.\n\\item\tRun a PERMANOVA using \\texttt{adonis}, use 1000 permutations and Euclidean distance.\n\\item\tChange the order that the main effects are prescribed in the model, and rerun the PERMANOVA.  Do the sums of squares for the main effects change while the sum of squares for the interaction stays the same?  Why is this?  Is there a way around this?  \n\\item\tRun PERMANOVA using adonis, use 1000 permutations and a Bray-Curtis distance matrix.\n\\item\tWhich approach, the one in c) or in e) is more appropriate?\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\n\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n \\bibliography{MMEbib}\n \\bibliographystyle{MMEbst}\n \n\\afterpage{\\null\\newpage} \n \\thispagestyle{empty}\n\\afterpage{\\null\\newpage}\n\n\\end{document}\n\n\n\n",
